{"headline":"Chest made a strong leap this week — keep building on that momentum across all lifts.","summary":"This was a solid training period with 28 sets logged across six muscle groups and over 10,700 kg of total tonnage moved. The standout was chest, which saw a meaningful jump in strength index, and back continued its gradual upward trend. Legs, shoulders, biceps, and triceps held steady, which is fine for maintenance but signals an opportunity to push a little harder in the weeks ahead. Volume distribution is reasonably balanced, and there are no glaring gaps in consistency. Overall, this is a healthy, productive week to build on.","progressiveOverload":{"status":"on_track","notes":"Chest showed the most notable progress this period, jumping from a strength index of 104 to 108 — a clear sign that intensity or load increased meaningfully there. Back also edged forward from 103 to 104, continuing the steady climb noted in the previous period. Legs (105), shoulders (102), biceps (101), and triceps (103) all remained flat compared to last period, suggesting these groups are being maintained rather than actively driven forward. Progressive overload is on track overall, but the stagnation in legs, shoulders, biceps, and triceps is worth addressing if the goal is continued strength development across the board."},"muscleImbalances":[{"muscleGroup":"shoulders","severity":"mild","observation":"Shoulders account for only 10.4% of volume split and showed no strength index gain, while chest and back — which they support — continue to grow. A mild imbalance risk exists if shoulder development falls further behind the pressing and pulling muscles."},{"muscleGroup":"biceps","severity":"mild","observation":"Biceps hold the lowest volume share at 4% and have a flat strength index. While direct bicep work is often supplemented by back training, the gap between back volume (28%) and bicep volume (4%) is wide enough to monitor."}],"deloadRecommended":false,"deloadReason":null,"actionItems":["Try to push leg intensity next period — since the legs strength index has been flat for at least two periods, consider adding a small amount of load or an extra rep to your main leg movements to reignite progress.","Give shoulders a deliberate nudge by adding one working set or slightly increasing the load on your primary shoulder exercise, as both volume share and strength index suggest this group is being undertrained relative to chest and back.","Maintain the chest momentum by continuing whatever you changed this week — whether that was heavier loads, better technique, or more focused effort — and aim for another small strength index gain next period.","Keep back progression rolling with its current approach, as the consistent, incremental gains there are exactly the kind of sustainable progress worth protecting."],"contextSummary":"Chest surged (index 104 to 108) and back crept forward (103 to 104), but legs, shoulders, biceps, and triceps were all flat — next period should focus on nudging those stagnant groups forward while protecting chest and back momentum."}